[FINAL-TEXT] === DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: false
faulty_devices: none
diagnosis_text:
The fabric is healthy and the “rack cut off” report does not match current state. End-to-end reachability between racks is working: pc_0_0 (10.0.0.2) successfully pings pc_0_1 (10.0.1.2) both directions with 0% loss and ~1 ms RTT. The top-of-rack leaf for the remote rack (leaf_router_0_1) has both upstream links operational: interfaces eth0 (to spine_router_0_0) and eth1 (to spine_router_0_1) report Link detected: yes at 10G, and are addressed as 172.16.0.7/31 and 172.16.0.11/31 respectively. On both spines, the corresponding downlink interfaces are UP with the expected addresses.

Control-plane is converged: BGP on leaf_router_0_1 shows two established neighbors (172.16.0.6 AS 65100 and 172.16.0.10 AS 65101) receiving prefixes and exchanging routes; leaf_router_0_0 and both spines show consistent BGP adjacencies as well. With host subnets advertised, traffic between 10.0.0.0/24 and 10.0.1.0/24 forwards normally via ECMP across the spines, which explains the successful probes and absence of timeouts.

Localization: no failing interfaces or daemons were found; specifically, leaf_router_0_1 uplinks (eth0/eth1) and spine-facing BGP sessions are UP. Root cause: no current fault; the earlier report was likely a transient link flap already cleared or a miscommunication.

Corrective fix: none required. If users still see timeouts, check host-level firewalls or application issues and capture timestamps to correlate with any future transient link events. Continuous monitoring is recommended. 
=== END ===